[]{#sec.oc label="sec.oc"}

Let $K$ be a knot in $S^3$ and $D$ be a knot diagram of $K$. Viewing $D$ as an embedded 4-valent graph in $\mathbbm R^2$, a *segment* refers to an edge of the graph and a *region* refers to a connected component of the complement of the graph. If we add over/under-pass information at every crossing as usual, the graph splits up into intervals, which we call *over-arcs*. Reversing all the over/under-pass information, the graph splits up into different intervals, which we call *under-arcs*.

